INT. ST. CLEMENT'S HOSPITAL - LAUNDRY CHUTE LANDING - NIGHT

A cramped steel landing at the bottom of the laundry chute, lit by one humming tube. Canvas bins. Stacks of folded sheets. A radiator ticks.

DR. HELEN VOSS, 40s, rumpled white coat, crouches beside a bin. Her fingers are on a patient's wrist.

NURSE AGNES BRANDT, 50s, thick glasses, kneels across from her with a penlight. Between them, on a pile of sheets, lies an unconscious elderly woman in a hospital gown.

                    HELEN
Pulse is thready.

                    AGNES
She came down the chute?

                    HELEN
Three floors. Bin broke her fall.

She presses two fingers to the woman's neck. Leans in close.

                    AGNES
How long has she been missing?

                    HELEN
Since the evening round.

Agnes lifts the old woman's eyelid. Shines the light. The pupil shrinks.

                    AGNES
She's responding.

                    HELEN
Good.

She starts to rise. The old woman groans. Helen drops back down.

CECILIA MARCH, 80s, wild white hair, gown twisted around her, opens bleary eyes. Her gaze goes from one face to the other.

                    CECILIA
Is it Tuesday?

                    HELEN
It's Thursday, Mrs. March.

                    CECILIA
I have to feed the hens.

Agnes shoots Helen a worried glance. Helen shakes her head, a small signal. Wait.

She takes the old woman's hand. Squeezes.

Cecilia squeezes back, strong. Too strong. Her fingers lock.

                    CECILIA (CONT'D)
They'll peck each other if I don't.

Helen tries to pull free. Can't. Agnes reaches to help. Cecilia's other hand snaps out and catches her wrist.

Both women freeze.

                    AGNES
Mrs. March, please let go.

                    CECILIA
Where's my husband?

She sits up. Fast. The sheets slide off. Her eyes are suddenly very clear.

                    HELEN
He's not here.

                    CECILIA
He promised he'd come.

She pushes herself to her feet. Helen scrambles back. Agnes grabs for the old woman's arm and gets a handful of gown.

The radiator bangs. Cecilia flinches. Her grip loosens.

She turns toward the sound. Stares at it. Her shoulders sag.

                    CECILIA (CONT'D)
(small)
He's been dead nine years, hasn't he.

The two women exchange a look. Neither answers.

She sits down again, slowly, on the pile of sheets, and puts her hands in her lap. She seems to shrink.

Agnes touches her shoulder, gently now. Helen exhales, long and shaky, and presses the call button on the wall.

                    HELEN
(into the intercom)
Gurney to laundry level. Patient found. Stable.

She releases the button. Looks down at the old woman. Smiles, tired.

Cecilia pats her hand. Almost motherly.
